{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"Lectures meet 3 sessions per week, 1.5 hours per session.","source_span":"Lectures: 3 sessions / week, 1.5 hours / session"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"Recitations meet 1 session per week, 1 hour per session.","source_span":"Recitations: 1 session / week, 1 hour / session"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"Students use the Java programming language to complete weekly software assignments.","source_span":"Students use Java™ programming language to complete weekly software assignments."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"If a student misses a lecture, they must still complete and hand in the active learning exercises by 7pm on the day of that lecture.","source_span":"you must still do and hand in the active learning exercises by 7pm on the day of lecture"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"Solutions to lecture exercises and solution Java files are posted about three hours after lecture on the same day.","source_span":"solution Java™ files are posted about three hours after lecture on the same day"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"Recitation sections have up to 12 students, and attendance is mandatory.","source_span":"Sections have up to 12 students. Attendance is mandatory."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"The prerequisite for this course is 18.01 (Single Variable Calculus).","source_span":"18.01 (Single Variable Calculus)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"Graduate students must register for 1.001 (a 9-unit subject) or 1.002 (for 12 units) instead of 1.00.","source_span":"1.001 is a 9 unit “G” subject. If you are a grad student and need 12 units, you may register for 1.002."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"The recommended textbook is Big Java by Cay Horstmann, 4th edition, Wiley, 2009, ISBN 9780470509487.","source_span":"Horstmann, Cay.\nBig Java\n. 4th ed. Wiley, 2009. ISBN: 9780470509487."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"The optional textbook is Numerical Recipes in C: The Art of Scientific Computing by Press, Teukolsky, et al., Cambridge University Press, 1992, ISBN 9780521437202.","source_span":"Numerical Recipes in C: The Art of Scientific Computing\n. Cambridge University Press, 1992. ISBN: 9780521437202."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"A laptop with at least 1 GB of memory running Windows Vista, Windows 7, Mac OSX, or Linux is needed to run the 1.00 software.","source_span":"A laptop with at least 1 GB of memory running Windows Vista, Windows 7, Mac OSX or Linux is needed to run the 1.00 software."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"10 Problem Sets, each worth 4 points, count for 40% of the final grade.","source_span":"10 Problem Sets (each worth 4 points)\n40%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"2 In-class Quizzes (open book, open notes), each worth 12 points, count for 24% of the final grade.","source_span":"2 In-class Quizzes (open book, open notes) (each worth 12 points)\n24%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"The Final Exam during Finals Period (open book, open notes), worth 20 points, counts for 20% of the final grade.","source_span":"Final Exam during Finals Period (open book, open notes) (worth 20 points)\n20%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"30 Active Learning Exercise Sessions, each worth 1/3 of a point, count for 10% of the final grade.","source_span":"30 Active Learning Exercise Sessions (each worth 1/3 of a point)\n10%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"Weekly Recitations, graded on attendance and participation, count for 6% of the final grade.","source_span":"Weekly Recitations, Graded on Attendance and Participation\n6%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"A student who violates the academic honesty guidelines may, at the discretion of the instructor-in-charge, receive an F as their final grade in 1.00/1.001/1.002.","source_span":"may, at the discretion of the instructor-in-charge, receive an F as their final grade in 1.00/1.001/1.002"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","statement":"Graded problem sets and quizzes are returned in recitation about 10 days after they are submitted.","source_span":"Graded problem sets and quizzes will be returned in recitation about 10 days after they are submitted."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"How many lecture and recitation sessions per week does this course have, and how long is each?","answer":"Lectures meet 3 sessions per week for 1.5 hours each, and recitations meet 1 session per week for 1 hour."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What is the prerequisite for MIT course 1.00?","answer":"18.01 (Single Variable Calculus)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What must a graduate student register for instead of 1.00, and how many units does each option carry?","answer":"Graduate students must register for 1.001 (9 units) or, if they need 12 units, 1.002."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What is the recommended textbook for this course, including edition, publisher, and ISBN?","answer":"Big Java by Cay Horstmann, 4th edition, Wiley, 2009, ISBN 9780470509487."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","fact_refs":[11,12,13,14,15],"question":"How is the final grade broken down among problem sets, quizzes, the final exam, active learning exercises, and recitations?","answer":"Problem sets are worth 40%, in-class quizzes 24%, the final exam 20%, active learning exercise sessions 10%, and weekly recitations 6%."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_1-00-introduction-to-computers-and-engin_syllabus","fact_refs":[16],"question":"What can happen to a student who violates the academic honesty guidelines in course 1.00?","answer":"They may, at the discretion of the instructor-in-charge, receive an F as their final grade in 1.00/1.001/1.002."}
